  <data name="Mod_home_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Green domains: Kernel and Monsters. Blue domains: Battle, String, Macro, Event, and Extras read-only hubs. Yellow domains: Encounter routing, AI write paths, Event recompilation, Maps, and companion-file decoders.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_home_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Workspace Overview</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_home_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>This shell is the staging ground for FFX kernel authoring, battle exploration, string and macro inspection, monster editing, future encounter and AI research, plus read-only Extras surfaces for PS2/PS3 knowledge.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_home_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Dashboard</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_home_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Start with Commands, Items, Monster Commands 1/2, Monster Editor, Battle Explorer, String Explorer, Macro Explorer, Event Explorer, and the new Extras read-only shell. Those are the strongest surfaces already proven by the current stack.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_monster_editor_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Primary write path for monster data.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_monster_editor_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Monster Editor</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_monster_editor_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Browse and edit the FFX monster species files with strong control over stats, loot, resistances, and structural data.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_monster_editor_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Writable</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_monster_editor_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Backed by the current FFXProjectEditor monster file flow. Best candidate for heavy authoring work.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_commands_hub_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Writers: command.bin (comandos de personagem), monmagic1.bin e monmagic2.bin (comandos exclusivos de monstro).</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_commands_hub_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Battle Commands</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_commands_hub_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Comandos/magias de personagem e os dois bancos de comandos de monstro — uma só superfície, três sub-abas.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_commands_hub_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Battle Commands hub</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_commands_hub_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cada sub-aba edita uma tabela de comando do kernel (command.bin / monmagic1.bin / monmagic2.bin) via o mesmo editor de sempre. A pílula de modo da sub-aba ativa é o sinal autoritativo.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_items_hub_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Writers: item.bin, takara.bin, buki_get.bin, shop slot payload, prepare.bin. Key Items ainda guard.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_items_hub_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Items</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_items_hub_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Itens, key items, baús/tesouros, recompensas de equipamento, lojas e a Mix Table — tudo numa superfície.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_items_hub_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Items hub</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_items_hub_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Items=item.bin · Key Items=important.bin (guard) · Treasures=takara.bin · Gear Rewards=buki_get.bin (writable) · Shop=slot payload guardado · Mix Table=prepare.bin. A pílula da sub-aba ativa é o sinal autoritativo.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_customizations_hub_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Writers: kaizou.bin, sum_grow.bin, a_ability.bin, arms_rate.bin.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_customizations_hub_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Customizations / Aeons</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_customizations_hub_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Customization de equipamento, aeon grow/teach e auto-abilities — uma superfície, duas sub-abas.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_customizations_hub_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Customizations hub</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_customizations_hub_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Customization / Aeons = writer de kaizou.bin + sum_grow.bin (com abas internas Gear / Aeon Grow); Auto-Abilities = writer de a_ability.bin + arms_rate.bin atrás de um self-check RT0. A pílula da sub-aba ativa é o sinal autoritativo.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_stats_hub_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Writers: ply_save.bin, ply_rom.bin, ctb_base.bin.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_stats_hub_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Stats</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_stats_hub_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Stats/growth dos personagens e a tabela CTB de timing de batalha — uma superfície, duas sub-abas.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_stats_hub_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Stats hub</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_stats_hub_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>PC Stats / Growth = writer de ply_save.bin + ply_rom.bin (stats base + curva de growth); CTB Base = writer de ctb_base.bin (agilidade→tickspeed). A pílula da sub-aba ativa é o sinal autoritativo.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_enemy_design_hub_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Writers: scripts de IA em monster_*.bin, clones de m###.bin (novo slot), e escala em massa de m###.bin.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_enemy_design_hub_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Enemy Design</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_enemy_design_hub_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>AI de monstro, criação de boss custom e o director de dificuldade — uma superfície, três sub-abas.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_enemy_design_hub_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Enemy Design hub</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_enemy_design_hub_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Monster AI Editor = editor de comportamento e DevKit tecnico do script ATEL; Custom Boss Creator = clona um m###.bin pra um novo slot (stats/nome/ModelId); Difficulty Director = escala stats em todos os m###.bin. A pilula de modo da sub-aba ativa e o sinal autoritativo.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_sphere_grid_hub_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Explorer + Panel pedem projeto; Builder/Canvas abrem sem projeto (gravar no projeto precisa de projeto).</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_sphere_grid_hub_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sphere Grid</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_sphere_grid_hub_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Explorer, Panel, Builder e Canvas — uma superfície, quatro sub-abas.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_sphere_grid_hub_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sphere Grid hub</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_sphere_grid_hub_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Explorer = sphere/panel/abmap + layout content; Panel = panel.bin grow/restore/edit; Builder/Canvas = topologia byte-safe offline (in-game NÃO-provado).</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_text_hub_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Writers: w_name.bin, btl_txt.bin. String/Macro/Event seguem read-only.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_text_hub_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Texto / Referência</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_text_hub_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>String, macro, weapon names, battle text e event — uma superfície, cinco sub-abas.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_text_hub_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Texto / Referência hub</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_text_hub_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>String/Macro/Event = exploradores read-only; Weapon Names (w_name.bin) e Battle Text (btl_txt.bin) = writers de texto seguros (RT0 provado). A pílula da sub-aba ativa é o sinal autoritativo.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_blitzball_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Writers edit bltz0002.ebp (roster stat-growth), the recruitment .ebp scripts, takara.bin (prize pool) and bltz0200.ebp (prize table + odds). The Atlas tab is read-only with no game-file writes.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_blitzball_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Blitzball</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_blitzball_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Roster, recruits, prize pool, prize table and the read-only prize Atlas — one surface, five sub-tabs.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_blitzball_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Atlas + Writer Lab</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_blitzball_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Four writer-lab editors (game-file edits, RT0 byte-identity proven / in-game RT2 pending) plus a read-only Spira Data Atlas catalog. The active sub-tab's mode pill is the authoritative read-only vs writer signal.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_save_editor_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Writer: Character stats (18 slots). PSU + raw PS2 + PC .ffx supported. RT0: --ffx-save-rt0 on 25848-byte files.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_save_editor_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Save Editor</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_save_editor_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Edit FFX playthrough save files (.psu, raw 25848-byte blobs, PC .ffx). Native port of FFXED v0.749 — Character tab first; other sections via FFXED.jar launcher until ported.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_save_editor_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Save Writer</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_save_editor_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Independent from the kernel workspace: point at a real save on disk. Checksum + tamper tag recalculated on save (dagal/FFXED algorithm). Equipment/Items/Blitzball/Sphere Grid/Minigame/Misc still route to bundled FFXED.jar.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_encounters_hub_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Writer: os 8 slots de monstro do btl_* (Formation Editor). Encounter Table segue read-only (sem save de tabela exposto).</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_encounters_hub_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Encounters &amp; Formation</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_encounters_hub_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Roteamento de encontros (btl.bin) e a formação de 8 monstros de cada btl_* — o par natural, numa superfície, duas sub-abas.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_encounters_hub_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Encounters &amp; Formation hub</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_encounters_hub_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Encounter Table = leitura read-only da tabela de roteamento (mapa→grupo→battle id; double-click abre a cena no Aurora Chamber); Formation Editor = writer BYTE-SAFE slot-only que troca os 8 monstros do btl_* (gate RT0 858/858). A pílula da sub-aba ativa é o sinal autoritativo.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_chamber_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Offline / read-only. Scene picker + render rodam sem projeto (precisam do ps3data extraido); a leitura/exportacao de ancoras exige um master/ carregado. Coordenadas sao battle-local cru, nao engine-exato.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_chamber_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Aurora Chamber</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_chamber_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Une a BIANCA (catalogo das 25 cenas btlmap) ao MapViewer: escolha uma cena -&gt; renderize a geometria HD -&gt; pegue as coordenadas dos atores (chunk3). Para a Aurora e a Bianca.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_chamber_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Read-Only Chamber</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_chamber_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Camada 3 da Aurora Chamber. CONSOME o MapViewer + a BIANCA como dependencia read-only (deep-link via ?catalog/?map; nunca toca o catalog.json compartilhado nem reescreve o app.js). Ponte cena (EncounterTable map -&gt; btlmap) = PROVADA; transform battle-&gt;mundo = HIPOTESE/UNCALIBRATED. Nenhum writer, nenhum probe; SPIRA FORGE permanece parado.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_field_explorer_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Field picker + render offline com ps3data; encounter list exige master/ (btl.bin). Nenhum writer.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_field_explorer_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Aurora Field Explorer</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_field_explorer_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Explore mapas overworld (map/) no MapViewer e veja encontros roteados pelo btl.bin para aquele field — modulo separado da Aurora Chamber (arenas btlmap).</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_field_explorer_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Read-Only Explorer</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_field_explorer_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Fase 1: picker de ~299 fields HD, export Phyre on-demand, painel btl.bin (grupos/danger/battleId) + overlay MapViewer. Demarcacao espacial de zonas de encontro no chao = RE pendente (mapout.vpa).</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_map_scene_editor_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Lab editável (sidecar, sem reserializar o glTF). Round-trip durável pro JOGO segue fronteira (só textura tem writer byte-exato provado). 'Open in Browser' = fallback.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_map_scene_editor_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Spira Forge · Map Scene Editor</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_map_scene_editor_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Editar a cena de mapa (RuntimeTools/FFXMapViewerWeb, 299 mapas) DENTRO do editor via WebView2: selecionar submesh por clique, mover com gizmo, painel de material/luz, edits no sidecar map-edits.json.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_map_scene_editor_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Embedded Scene Lab</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_map_scene_editor_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Hospeda o lab de cena de mapa editável in-process. Servidor http local (python http.server 127.0.0.1:8768) serve o repo-root pro catálogo + glTF dos mapas resolverem; o WebView2 Evergreen renderiza no painel.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_explorer_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Read-heavy today. Formation editing comes next; full encounter routing and ATEL authoring stay controlled.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_explorer_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Battle Explorer</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_explorer_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Inspect battle files as real multi-chunk assets: ATEL script, worker mapping, formation, positions, and encounter-table footprint.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_explorer_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Explorer</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_explorer_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>This is the first serious read-heavy bridge into encounter work. Safe focus: inspect structures, confirm references, and prepare the future formation editor.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_runtime_dll_manager_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Controls game-root proxy DLLs and modules\\*.dll. Runtime proof comes from process modules plus FFXProbeBlock_v1 / FFXHooksBlock_v1 where available.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_runtime_dll_manager_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Injected DLLs</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_runtime_dll_manager_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Show which runtime DLLs are installed, disabled, or currently visible in FFX, then stage on/off changes for the next game boot.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_runtime_dll_manager_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Runtime Switchboard</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_runtime_dll_manager_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Jarvis is keeping this honest: file toggles arm the loader by renaming/copying DLLs; already loaded code stays alive until FFX restarts.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_live_battle_lab_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Read-heavy plus proven runtime writes. Good for rapid test loops against Commands, Items, MonMagic, Auto-Abilities, Customizations, and Aeon Grow.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_live_battle_lab_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Live Battle Lab</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_live_battle_lab_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Inspect the live BTL runtime, reload proven file domains straight into memory, and prepare force-battle work without guessing at ATEL or routing writes.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_live_battle_lab_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Runtime Lab</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_live_battle_lab_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Jarvis is only exposing the pieces already proven by this codebase: battle-state inspection, enemy runtime snapshots, in-memory reload of known buffers, and BTL debug flags. Force Battle stays experimental until its trigger path is genuinely trusted.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_overlay_lab_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Writes only module-loader config files under the game modules folder. The running DLL reads these on startup; restart the game after changing the config.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_overlay_lab_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Aurora Overlay Lab</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_overlay_lab_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Configure the ffx-hooks.dll Aurora W2S/texture overlay from the editor, writing persistent modules\\config flags and aurora_overlay.ini for the next game boot.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_overlay_lab_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Runtime Product Lab</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_aurora_overlay_lab_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>This productizes the proved W2S/D3D11 overlay as an operable switchboard. It does not rename the native renderer or promote the IDA owner candidates beyond their current evidence.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_tracker_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Live-game helper. Read-heavy.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_tracker_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Battle Tracker</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_tracker_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Inspect live battle-state data while the game is running and compare runtime values against file-authored expectations.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_tracker_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Runtime</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_battle_tracker_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Best used as telemetry and verification, not as the foundation of the authoring pipeline.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_inventory_tracker_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Live-game helper. Read-heavy.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_inventory_tracker_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Inventory Tracker</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_inventory_tracker_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Inspect runtime inventory state and quickly validate item-table effects or progression-related test cases.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_inventory_tracker_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Runtime</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_inventory_tracker_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Good for debug loops and validating kernel-side edits.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_arena_tracker_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Live-game helper. Read-heavy.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_arena_tracker_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Arena Tracker</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_arena_tracker_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Track monster arena progress and runtime state during real gameplay sessions for research and verification.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_arena_tracker_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Runtime</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_arena_tracker_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Useful as an operational panel while authoring monster and encounter changes elsewhere.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_debug_menu_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Live-game helper. Not the canonical save path.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_debug_menu_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Debug Menu</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_debug_menu_Description" xml:space="preserve"><value>Live runtime toggles and save-adjacent debugging helpers for rapid inspection work against the running game.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_debug_menu_Mode" xml:space="preserve"><value>Runtime</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_debug_menu_Notes" xml:space="preserve"><value>Useful for fast experiments, but runtime editing should not replace file-authored workflows.</value></data>
  <data name="Mod_textures_tm2_Scope" xml:space="preserve"><value>Read-only only. Native preview is limited to the proved indexed TIM2 cohort; experimental, metadata-only, and blocked states stay explicit.</value></data>
